86159283 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b00e261-c224-51e6-96af-829d46ca11d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161902 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161902 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/399738d7c4da59c73c954cfd41d8c76fc93ad0351fc8f2854ad33796aa2379df.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161902 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0a12130-2908-58fd-a8e0-91083fa30e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161902 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=U \\Sigma V ^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$ \n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/f6/f6703dcf594334ec5ab821bbaac5654de5e3970b0e7981f256a8fd00df31060f.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161903 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161903 #edb/ref 1071 "For the matrix \n![](images/1d/1d0733f2c724253c804c596e1d0a1432b8281afa7a14a9dc6702b099ab4795e0.svg) find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64e7f84f-da34-5ae2-b2d1-72373a1d2888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161903 #edb/ref 1074 "For an \"almost\" zero $m \\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n \\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{+} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,{4}^{-1} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186161904 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161904 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fdc735895573e0d7c1eb7865233dd7b56a38aeeb9c76bba77fa669212262791d.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161904 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/d7/d750c1c5030e0d1df7e8b14790e3f189e69346ca392d3d2885ad49db0e66b7e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186161902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162089 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162090 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162090 #edb/ref 1038 #